A||||11||Don Roger||Porsche spyder-style body on Fiero?||||1||
Z||000000||Don Roger||08-16-2001||11:54 PM||||I posted this note to one of the kit car magazines, and the answer was a bit vague and mentioned someone who had ended up making a new frame to accomodate the Spyder replica shell and the Fiero suspension, hardware and engine. Any feedback for a newby?<P>"I'm a big fan of the classic Porsche Spyder 550 - RS60 style of  sports racing cars and noticed in past buyer's guides that the wheelbases of the Porsches and the Fiero are about the same; but the Fiero re-bodies are about 78 to 80 inches wide while the spyder bodies are advertised as between 60 and 66 inches wide.<P>How much of the extra width noted of the  traditional Fiero kits is "overhang"?  Since my plan is not to build an accurate replica, I wouldn't be worried about making flared fenders; but how can I determine if the Fiero skeleton would fit underneath a Spyder-replica body?  I know one builder has widened a similar shell to fit over a Lotus skeleton, but what about cowl location etc?<P>It seems to me that the Fiero skeleton provides a better basic suspension, brake system and drive train than a traditional VW floor pan."<P>Don in Canada<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||08-17-2001||08:44 AM||||Don,<P>Welcome to the list. I don't really have your answers but wanted to mention that in this months issue of Kit Car magazine, there is a picture of Fiero/Boxster conversion. Looks like it's in the very early stages of development but shows promise.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
